Id: no-sleep-in-a-test
Pattern: (Thread\.Sleep\(|Task\.Delay\(|SpinWait\.SpinUntil\()
Subject: *Tests/*.cs
Except: none
Issue: 47

A test that waits for real time to pass is measuring the runner rather than the
plugin. It passes on a quiet machine, fails on a loaded one, gets a longer wait
put on it, and ends up quarantined; the code it covered is then covered on paper
and by nothing in fact.

The replacement is ClockATestAdvances. A test that needs three hours to have
passed advances three hours and asserts immediately, which also makes the
boundary reachable: one tick before a deadline and one tick after it cannot be
distinguished by any test that got there by waiting.

The subject is written as a directory suffix rather than as the test project's
current path, because that path still carries the template's name and #14
renames it. A rule naming the old path would go quiet on the day of the rename
instead of failing.

Its bound is the same one every rule here has. An await on a task that sleeps
somewhere else, or a delay assembled at run time, is invisible to a pattern that
reads one line at a time.
